    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2655.3,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 606,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHowever, to humans, Earth, and all known life, water is much more than just a chemical formula. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it matters, and its unique properties:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Physical Substance\nIn the physical world, water is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct states of matter:\n*   **Liquid:** What we normally drink and what fills our oceans, rivers, and lakes.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Found in glaciers, snow, ice cubes, and polar ice caps.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Found in the air as humidity, invisible water vapor, and clouds.\n\n### 2. The \"Elixir of Life\"\nWater is the fundamental building block of all known life. \n*   **For the Earth:** About **71% of the Earth’s surface** is covered in water, mostly in the oceans (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). It shapes our geography, drives our weather systems, and regulates the global climate.\n*   **For Living Organisms:** The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. Water is essential for survival because it acts as a solvent—it dissolves and carries nutrients, minerals, and chemicals through the body, regulates body temperature, and flushes out waste. Without water, cells would die, and biological processes would cease.\n\n### 3. Unique Scientific Properties\nWater behaves very strangely compared to other liquids, and these quirks make life possible:\n*   **The Universal Solvent:** Because of its chemical structure, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it carries vital nutrients in our bodies and minerals through the soil.\n*   **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, **expands** and becomes *less* dense when it turns to ice. This is why ice cubes float. In nature, this is crucial: lakes freeze from the top down, creating an insulating layer of ice that allows aquatic life to survive the winter underneath.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This prevents the oceans (and our bodies) from boiling or freezing too quickly, stabilizing the climate of the entire planet.\n\n### Summary\nWater is a transparent, odorless, and tasteless liquid (in its pure form) that covers most of our planet and fuels every living thing. It is simultaneously the most common molecule in our daily lives and one of the most miraculous substances in the universe."
